BOUNDARY: The refutation’s witness is the alternating series (Grandi series) with grouping \(n_j = e_j\) (the even-index map). Checked: (i) The original series diverges—its terms are \(\pm 1\) and do not tend to \(0\), so by the contrapositive of `lem-nth-term-test` the series cannot converge. (ii) The grouping satisfies the hypothesis of the false claim: \(n_0 = e_0 = 0\), \(n_j\) is strictly increasing, and the blocks \(B_j = \sum_{k=n_j}^{n_{j+1}-1} a_k\) cover all indices exactly once (each block consists of the two consecutive indices \(e_j\) and \(e_j+1 = o_j\)). (iii) Every block \(B_j = 1 + (-1) = 0\); thus the series of blocks converges to \(0\). Hence the false claim’s antecedent holds while its consequent fails. The remark about a different grouping of the same series (n = (0,1,3,5,…)) yielding sum \(1\) is also correct and does not affect the counterexample. The false claim is stated without extra hypotheses such as \(a_k \to 0\) or bounded block length, so the witness is admissible. All edge cases (empty sum, index shifts) are handled consistently with the cited definitions.

